-- [F01-M0051] STR_TO_DATE
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/100.date-and-time-functions-of-mysql-mode/3600.str-to-date-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/100.date-and-time-functions-of-mysql-mode/3600.str-to-date-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: STR_TO_DATE
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   STR_TO_DATE() converts str into a DATETIME, DATE, or TIME value based on format. The return value type depends on the parts of the date and time that are included in format.
--   • If str or format is NULL, this function returns NULL.
--   • If str is not a valid date, the return value varies with the settings of sql_mode:
--   • If sql_mode contains NO_ZERO_IN_DATE, NULL is returned.
--   • If sql_mode does not contain NO_ZERO_IN_DATE:
--   • If str is an integer, and str \< 0, it returns 0000-00-00 00:00:00 and displays a warning prompt.
--   • If str is an integer and 0 ≤ str ≤ 69, it returns the year of the sum of 1999-11-30 00:00:00 plus str.
--   • If str is an integer and 69 \< str ≤ 99, it returns the year of the result of 1969-11-30 00:00:00 plus str minus 70.
--   • If str is an integer and 100 \< str ≤ 9999, it returns the year of the result of 0099-11-30 00:00:00 plus str minus 100.
--   • If str is an integer, and str \> 10000, it returns NULL and displays a warning prompt.
--   • If str is a string, the function converts the string to a datetime if possible, and returns the result of the datetime value of str plus 1999-11-30 00:00:00. If str cannot be converted to a datetime, NULL is returned.
--   • If str is neither an integer nor a string, it returns NULL and displays a warning prompt.
--   For more information about the formats supported by format, see DATE_FORMAT.
-- Documented SQL syntax: no separate SQL syntax block on this source page; see notes and source.
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT STR_TO_DATE(-100,'%Y-%b-%D %r');
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT STR_TO_DATE(0,'%Y-%b-%D %r');
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT STR_TO_DATE(88,'%Y-%b-%D %r');
-- Example 4: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT STR_TO_DATE(2088,'%Y-%b-%D %r');
-- Example 5: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT STR_TO_DATE(10001,'%Y-%b-%D %r');
-- Example 6: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT STR_TO_DATE('a2014-Jan-1st 5:5:5 pm', '%Y-%b-%D %r');
-- Example 7: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT STR_TO_DATE('a2014-Jan-1st 5:5:5 pm', 'a%Y-%b-%D %r');
-- Example 8: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT STR_TO_DATE(1.6,'%Y-%b-%D %r');
-- Example 9: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT STR_TO_DATE(pi(),'%Y-%b-%D %r');

-- [F01-M0052] SUBDATE
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/100.date-and-time-functions-of-mysql-mode/3700.subdate-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/100.date-and-time-functions-of-mysql-mode/3700.subdate-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: SUBDATE
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   When you call the SUBDATE() function by using INTERVAL as the second argument, the function is a synonym for DATE_SUB() and returns the value of date minus expr.
--   For more information about the INTERVAL and unit arguments, see DATE_ADD.
--   SUBDATE(expr,days) allows you to use an integer value for days. In this case, it returns the result of subtracting the specified number of days from the date or datetime expression expr.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   SUBDATE(date,INTERVAL expr unit)
--
--   SUBDATE(expr,days)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT DATE_SUB('2022-01-02', INTERVAL 31 DAY);
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT SUBDATE('2022-01-02', INTERVAL 31 DAY);
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT SUBDATE('2022-01-02 12:00:00', 31);

