-- [F01-M0095] INT2IP
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/3600.int2ip-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/3600.int2ip-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: INT2IP
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   INT2IP() converts the integer int_value into an IP address.
--   For example, the integer s is converted into the IP address s4.s3.s2.s1 based on the following rules:
--   • s1: s mod 2^8^
--   • s2: s - s1 / 2^8^ mod 2^8^
--   • s3: ((s - s1) / 2^8^ - s2) / 2^8^ mod 2^8^
--   • s4: (((s - s1) / 2^8^ - s2) / 2^8^ - s3) / 2^8^ mod 2^8^
--   If the entered value is NULL or greater than 4294967295, NULL is returned.
-- Documented SQL syntax: no separate SQL syntax block on this source page; see notes and source.
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT
     INT2IP(16777216),
     HEX(16777216),
     INT2IP(1)
     \G
*************************** 1. row ***************************
INT2IP(16777216): 1.0.0.0
   HEX(16777216): 1000000
       INT2IP(1): 0.0.0.1;

-- [F01-M0096] IP2INT
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/3700.ip2int-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/3700.ip2int-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: IP2INT
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   IP2INT() converts the IP address ip_addr into an integer.
--   For example, the IP address s1.s2.s3.s4 is converted based on the following rule:
--   s1 \ 2^24^ + s2 \ 2^16^ + s3 \* 2^8^ + s4
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   IP2INT('ip_addr')
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT
     IP2INT('0.0.0.1'),
     HEX(IP2INT('0.0.0.1')),
     HEX(IP2INT('1.0.0.0'))
     \G
*************************** 1. row ***************************
     IP2INT('0.0.0.1'): 1
HEX(IP2INT('0.0.0.1')): 1
HEX(IP2INT('1.0.0.0')): 1000000;

-- [F01-M0097] KEYVALUE
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/3750.keyvalue-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/3750.keyvalue-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: KEYVALUE
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   This function is used to extract the value corresponding to a specific key from a key-value string.
--   Note
--   For OceanBase Database V4.3.5, this function is supported starting from V4.3.5 BP1.
--   SECTION: Limitations and considerations
--   • You cannot specify delimiter_1 and delimiter_2 as the same string.
--   • If the string obtained by splitting with delimiter_1 contains multiple delimiter_2 strings, the first delimiter_2 is used as the delimiter to obtain the corresponding key and value. For example, executing SELECT KEYVALUE('name:Li:xiang;age:12', ';', ':', 'name'); returns the following result:
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   • str: The string containing multiple key-value pairs. Supported types: VARCHAR, TINYTEXT, TEXT, LONGTEXT, and NULL. Not supported: JSON.
--   • delimiter_1, delimiter_2: Optional. The delimiters. Details:
--   • delimiter_1: The delimiter for key-value pairs. Default value: ;. Supported types: VARCHAR and NULL.
--   • delimiter_2: The delimiter for keys and values. Default value: :. Supported types: VARCHAR and NULL.
--   • key: The key to be searched for (key). Supported types: VARCHAR and NULL. After splitting the string str using delimiter_1 and delimiter_2, the function returns the value corresponding to the specified key.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   • If any parameter in the expression is NULL, the result is NULL.
--   • If the corresponding key is not found, the function returns NULL.
--   • If multiple key-value pairs match, the function returns the value corresponding to the first matching key.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   KEYVALUE(str, [delimiter_1, delimiter_2,] key)
-- Official SELECT example: none available on this source page. Use the preserved signature in a SELECT statement.

-- [F01-M0098] CHAR
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/400.char-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/400.char-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: CHAR
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   CHAR() converts arguments to integers and return a string that consists of the code characters of the integers. Any NULL argument is skipped.
--   If an argument is greater than 255, the argument is converted into multiple bytes. For example, CHAR(256) is equivalent to CHAR(1,0), and CHAR(256*256) is equivalent to CHAR(1,0,0).
--   By default, CHAR() returns a string that uses the binary character set. You can use a USING clause to specify the character set for the return value.
--   If the return value is invalid in the specified character set, a WARNING is prompted. In particular, if sql_mode is set to STRICT_ALL_TABLES or STRICT_TRANS_TABLES, and the return value is invalid in the specified character set, NULL is returned.
--   For information about sql_mode, see sql_mode.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   CHAR(value1,... [USING charset_name])
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
select CHARSET(char('')), CHARSET(char('' USING utf8mb4));
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT CHAR(399 USING utf8);
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT CHAR(79,99,101,97,110,66,97.4,115,'101');

