-- [F01-M0107] MAKE_SET
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/5500.make-set-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/5500.make-set-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: MAKE_SET
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   MAKE_SET() returns a set of strings corresponding to bits whose value is 1. The strings are separated with commas (,). The str1 string corresponds to bit 2^0^, str2 to bit 2^1^, and so on. If the strn string does not exist, NULL is returned.
--   For example, if you set bits to 5, whose binary value is 0101, then str1 and str3 are returned.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   MAKE_SET(bits,str1,str2,...)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT MAKE_SET(1,'a','b','c');
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT MAKE_SET(1 | 4,'hello','ocean','base');
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT MAKE_SET(1 | 4,'hello','ocean',NULL,'base');

-- [F01-M0108] MAX_PT
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/5520.max-pt-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/5520.max-pt-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: MAX_PT
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   This function returns the maximum value of the partitioning key among the partitions that contain data in a partitioned table.
--   Note
--   This function is supported starting from OceanBase Database V4.4.2 BP3.
--   SECTION: Limitations and considerations
--   • The caller must have the SELECT privilege on the target table (MySQL-compatible mode checks the table-level privilege). Otherwise, the call fails.
--   • Table names in parameters must be constants (string literals that can be determined during parsing). Variables or non-constant expressions are not supported as table name parameters.
--   • The table to be queried must be a partitioned table with a partition type of RANGE or LIST.
--   • The table to be queried must be an internal table. External tables and DBLink are not supported.
--   • Only single-column partitioning keys are supported; multi-column partitioning keys are not supported.
--   • Expression partitioning is not supported. If the partitioning key is not a direct reference to a single column but an expression in the partition definition, MAX_PT cannot be used.
--   • For a subpartitioned table, only the values of the first-level partitions are returned.
--   • If all partitions are empty, NULL is returned.
--   • If different partition values exist in the same partition, the result of MAX_PT is undefined, and a value from any row in that partition will be returned.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   • database_name: Optional. The name of the database where the table is located. If not specified, the table is in the default database of the current session. You can also specify both the database and table names in the first string parameter in the 'db_name.table_name' format (separated by at most one .).
--   • table_name: The name of the table. The database and table names are resolved according to the current session's case-sensitivity policy and valid character rules, and must match the names stored in the data dictionary (including case).
--   SECTION: Return type
--   Returns the maximum partition value (compared by using the max() logic) among the partitions that contain data. The data type is the same as the partitioning key type.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   MAX_PT('[database_name.]table_name')
-- Official SELECT example: none available on this source page. Use the preserved signature in a SELECT statement.

-- [F01-M0109] MD5_CONCAT_WS
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/5550.md5-concat-ws-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/5550.md5-concat-ws-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: MD5_CONCAT_WS
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   This function concatenates multiple strings into one string, with the separator used to separate adjacent strings. If a NULL value is present in the parameters, it is replaced with the replace_null_value before concatenation. The MD5 hash of the concatenated string is then returned as the result.
--   Note
--   This function was introduced in V4.4.2 of the V4.4.x series. This function was introduced in V4.3.5 BP4 of the V4.3.5 series.
--   SECTION: Limitations and considerations
--   • This function may encounter hash collisions, leading to the same result for different inputs.
--   • You must specify a value that does not conflict with the input columns as the replacement for NULL. This ensures that the replacement value does not conflict with the input column values. The following example shows an incorrect usage:
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   • separator: The separator used to concatenate the input values. It is a string.
--   • replace_null_value: The value used to replace NULL values. It is a string.
--   • str1, ... , strN: The input values to be concatenated (variable parameters). They are a list of strings.
--   SECTION: Return value
--   The MD5 hash of the concatenated string (32-bit hexadecimal).
--   Notice
--   You must specify both separator and replace_null_value. If one of them is NULL, the function returns NULL.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   MD5_CONCAT_WS(separator, replace_null_value, str1, ... , strN)
-- Official SELECT example: none available on this source page. Use the preserved signature in a SELECT statement.

-- [F01-M0110] MID
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/5600.mid-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/5600.mid-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: MID
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   MID() returns a substring of str. The start position is pos, and the length of the substring is len characters. If any argument is NULL, the function returns NULL.
--   • If len is not specified, the returned substring starts from the position pos till the end of str.
--   • If the value of pos is negative, the start position is reversely determined from the end of str.
--   • If len is less than or equal to 0, or the start position specified by pos is invalid, an empty string is returned.
--   This function is the alias of the SUBSTRING(str,pos,len) function.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   MID(str,pos,len)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT MID('OceanBase',6);

