-- [F01-M0119] REGEXP_INSTR
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/7200.regexp-instr-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/7200.regexp-instr-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: REGEXP_INSTR
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   REGEXP_INSTR() returns the start position of the substring in the expr expression that matches the pat expression. If no such substrings exist, the function returns 0. If expr or pat is NULL, the function returns NULL. The return value of the position starts from 1.
--   • pos indicates the start position for character search in the expr expression. The default value is 1.
--   • occurrence specifies the ordinal number of occurrences of pat in expr. The default value is 1.
--   • return_option specifies the type of the return value. The default value is 0.
--   • The value 0 indicates that REGEXP_INSTR() returns the position of the first character of the substring that matches pat.
--   • The value 1 indicates that REGEXP_INSTR() returns the position of the last character of the substring that matches pat.
--   • match_type indicates the matching rule. For information about matching rules, see REGEXP_LIKE.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   REGEXP_INSTR(expr, pat [, pos [, occurrence [, return_option [, match_type ]]]])
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_INSTR('ocean base oceanbase', 'ocean');
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_INSTR('ocean base oceanbase', 'ocean',2);
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_INSTR('ocean base oceanbase', 'ocean',1,2);
-- Example 4: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_INSTR('ocean base oceanbase', 'ocean',1,2,1);

-- [F01-M0120] REGEXP_LIKE
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/7300.regexp-like-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/7300.regexp-like-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: REGEXP_LIKE
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   If the expr string matches the regular expression specified by pat, REGEXP_LIKE() returns 1. Otherwise, it returns 0. If expr or pat is NULL, NULL is returned.
--   match_type indicates the matching mode. The valid values are as follows:
--   • c indicates case-sensitive.
--   • i indicates case-insensitive.
--   • m indicates that line breaks in the string are recognized. By default, only line breaks at the beginning and end of the string expression are matched.
--   • n indicates that a period (.) only matches line breaks (\n). By default, a period (.) matches any single character that is not a line break (\n).
--   • u indicates that only Unix line ends are matched. Only the line break (\n) can be recognized as the line end by the period (.), caret (^), and dollar sign ($).
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   REGEXP_LIKE(expr, pat[, match_type])
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_LIKE('OceanBase', 'OCEANBASE');
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_LIKE('OceanBase', 'OCEANBASE','c');
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_LIKE('Ocean\nBase', 'OCEAN.BASE');
-- Example 4: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_LIKE('Ocean\nBase', 'OCEAN.BASE','n');

-- [F01-M0121] REGEXP_REPLACE
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/7400.regexp-replace-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/7400.regexp-replace-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: REGEXP_REPLACE
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   For substrings in the expr expression that match the pattern pat, REGEXP_REPLACE() replaces the substrings with repl and returns the resulting string. If str, pat, or repl is NULL, the function returns NULL.
--   • pos indicates the start position for character search in the expr expression. The default value is 1.
--   • occurrence indicates which substring that matches the pattern should be replaced with repl. The default value 0 indicates that all matching substrings are replaced.
--   • match_type indicates the matching rule. For information about matching rules, see REGEXP_LIKE.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   REGEXP_REPLACE(expr, pat, repl[, pos[, occurrence[, match_type]]])
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_REPLACE('OceanBase', 'a', '2');
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_REPLACE('OceanBase', 'a', '2',7);
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_REPLACE('OceanBase', 'a', '2',1,2);

-- [F01-M0122] REGEXP_SUBSTR
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/7500.regexp-substr-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/7500.regexp-substr-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: REGEXP_SUBSTR
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   REGEXP_SUBSTR() searches the str string for the substring that matches the regular expression specified by pattern. The function returns NULL if the substring does not exist. This function supports multi-byte characters. NULL is returned if any parameter other than match_param is set to NULL. The parameters are described as follows:
--   • str: the string to be searched. Multi-byte characters are supported.
--   • pattern: a regular expression. The regular expression rules are compatible with the MySQL database.
--   • position: the start position for the search. The value must be a positive integer, and an error is returned if the value is less than or equal to 0. NULL is returned if NULL is entered. The default value is 1, which specifies to search from the first character.
--   • occurrence: the ordinal number of occurrences at which the matched value is to be returned. The value must be a positive integer, and an error is returned if the value is less than or equal to 0. NULL is returned if NULL is entered. The default value is 1, which specifies to return the first matched result.
--   • match_param: the matching rule in the string type. For information about matching rules, see REGEXP_LIKE.
--   • subexpr: the ordinal number of the group in the regular expression whose value is to be returned. The value is an integer greater than or equal to 0. An error is returned if the value is less than 0. The default value is 0, which means to return the substring that matches the regular expression specified for pattern.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   REGEXP_SUBSTR(str,pattern,[position[,occurrence[,match_param[,subexpr]]]])
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_SUBSTR('I have 2 apples and 100 bucks!', '[[:blank:]][[:alnum:]]*', 1, 1) FROM DUAL;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_SUBSTR('foothebar', 'foo(.*)(bar)', 1, 1, 'c', 1) FROM DUAL;

