-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/8700.substring_index-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: SUBSTRING_INDEX
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   SUBSTRING_INDEX() returns a string from str before delim and count appear.
--   • If count is a positive value, the content to the left of the final delimiter (starting from the left) is returned.
--   • If count is a negative value, the content to the right of the delimiter (starting from the right) is returned.
--   • If any argument is NULL, the return value is NULL.
--   • If str or delim is an empty string, an empty string is returned.
--   • If count=0, an empty string is returned.
--   • str, delim, and count support implicit conversion between numeric values and strings.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   SUBSTRING_INDEX(str, delim, count)
-- Official SELECT example: none available on this source page. Use the preserved signature in a SELECT statement.

-- [F01-M0137] TRIM
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/8800.trim-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-mysql-mode/200.string-functions-of-mysql-mode/8800.trim-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: TRIM
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   TRIM() deletes all prefixes and/or suffixes of a string. The default value is BOTH. If the parameters include a NULL value, the function returns NULL.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   TRIM([[{BOTH | LEADING | TRAILING}] [remstr] FROM] str
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT
     TRIM(' bar ')
     TRIM(LEADING 'x' FROM 'xxxbarxxx')
     TRIM(BOTH 'x' FROM 'xxxbarxxx')
     TRIM(TRAILING 'x' FROM 'xxxbarxxx')
     \G
*************************** 1. row ***************************
                      TRIM(' bar '): bar
 TRIM(LEADING 'x' FROM 'xxxbarxxx'): barxxx
    TRIM(BOTH 'x' FROM 'xxxbarxxx'): bar
TRIM(TRAILING 'x' FROM 'xxxbarxxx'): xxxbar;

