-- [F01-M0221] GROUP_CONCAT
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/300.aggregate-function-of-mysql-mode/700.group-concat-agg-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/300.aggregate-function-of-mysql-mode/700.group-concat-agg-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: GROUP_CONCAT
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   Returns a string result with non-NULL values from a group, connected by a separator.
--   • ORDER BY specifies the sorting column, ASC indicates ascending order, and DESC indicates descending order. The default is ASC.
--   • SEPARATOR specifies the separator between connected strings. The default separator is ,.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   GROUP_CONCAT([DISTINCT] expr [,expr ...]
--                [ORDER BY {unsigned_integer | col_name | expr}
--                    [ASC | DESC] [,col_name ...]]
--                [SEPARATOR str_val])
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM book;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT GROUP_CONCAT(bookname) FROM book GROUP BY bookname;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
with unique book names
SELECT GROUP_CONCAT(distinct(bookname)) FROM book GROUP BY bookname;

-- [F01-M0222] GROUPING
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/300.aggregate-function-of-mysql-mode/800.grouping-agg-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/300.aggregate-function-of-mysql-mode/800.grouping-agg-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: GROUPING
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   When you use the GROUP BY clause, you can specify the WITH ROLLUP modifier to aggregate the groups. This represents higher-level (that is, super-aggregate) summary operations and returns extra rows. For example, you can execute the following SQL statement to group the sales profit by year and generate the sum of the groups.
--   The row whose year value is NULL is the extra super-aggregated row.
--   If you use WITH ROLLUP to modify multiple GROUP BY columns, the result is more complex. In this case, each group produces a super-aggregated row. The following sample SQL statement generates additional super-aggregated rows:
--   The GROUPING() function identifies the super-aggregated rows. If the row is super-aggregated, the function returns 1. Otherwise, it returns 0.
--   For example, you can use the GROUPING() function to rewrite the preceding sample SQL statement as follows:
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   GROUPING(expr)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT year, SUM(profit) AS profit
       FROM sales
       GROUP BY year WITH ROLLUP;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT year, country, product, SUM(profit) AS profit
       FROM sales
       GROUP BY year, country, product WITH ROLLUP;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT
         IF(GROUPING(year), 'All years', year) AS year,
         IF(GROUPING(country), 'All countries', country) AS country,
         IF(GROUPING(product), 'All products', product) AS product,
         SUM(profit) AS profit
       FROM sales
       GROUP BY year, country, product WITH ROLLUP;
-- Example 4: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT IF(GROUPING(manager_id),'manager_id',manager_id) AS manager_id,SUM(salary) FROM employees GROUP BY manager_id WITH ROLLUP;

