-- [F01-M0232] LEAD
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-mysql-mode/2900.lead-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-mysql-mode/2900.lead-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: LEAD
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   LEAD() retrieves the data of the Nth row after the current row for a specific field in a single query. While this operation can be achieved using self-joins of the same table, the LEAD() window function offers higher efficiency.
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   The following table describes the parameters of the function.
--   Parameter | Description
--   value_expr | The fields to be compared.
--   offset | The offset of value_expr.
--   default | The default return value. The default value NULL indicates that if you do not explicitly specify a default value, the return value is NULL.
--   [ { RESPECT \ | IGNORE } NULLS ] | Specifies whether to include NULLs in the result. The default value RESPECT NULLS specifies to include NULLs in the calculation.
--   order_by_clause | Specifies that the data must be sorted on a column to determine the ordinal number of rows.
--   query_partition_clause | Specifies the queried partition. If you do not specify the argument, all data rows are queried.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   LEAD { ( value_expr [, offset [, default]]) [ { RESPECT | IGNORE } NULLS ] | ( value_expr [ { RESPECT | IGNORE } NULLS ] [, offset [, default]] )} OVER ([ query_partition_clause ] order_by_clause)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT last_name, LEAD(salary) OVER(ORDER BY salary) lead, LAG(salary) OVER(ORDER BY salary) lag FROM exployees;

-- [F01-M0233] BIT_AND
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-mysql-mode/300.bit-and-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-mysql-mode/300.bit-and-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: BIT_AND
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   BIT_AND() returns the bitwise AND of all bits in expr.
--   The result is a binary string or a number. This depends on the type of the argument value. When the argument value contains a binary string and the argument is not a hexadecimal, bit, or NULL literal, a binary string result is calculated. Otherwise, a numeric result is calculated. If necessary, the function converts the argument value to an unsigned 64-bit integer.
--   If no matching row is found, BIT_AND() returns a neutral value whose all bits are set to 1. The neutral value has the same length as the argument value. NULL has no effect on the result unless all values are NULL. In this case, the result is a neutral value that has the same length as the argument value.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   BIT_AND(expr) OVER (analytic_clause)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM product_information;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT product_id, BIT_AND(min_price) OVER (partition by product_id) bits FROM product_information;

-- [F01-M0234] MAX
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-mysql-mode/3000.max-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-mysql-mode/3000.max-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: MAX
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   MAX() returns the maximum value in the specified data set.
--   The argument of MAX() can be a string, in which case the maximum string value is returned.
--   • The DISTINCT keyword can be used to retrieve the maximum of the distinct values of expr. However, the result is the same as omitting DISTINCT.
--   • The ALL keyword indicates to search all values of expr. By default, ALL is used.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   MAX([ DISTINCT | ALL ] expr) OVER (analytic_clause)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT last_name, MAX(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY job_id) max_s FROM employees;

