-- [F01-M0236] NTH_VALUE
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-mysql-mode/3200.nth-value-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-mysql-mode/3200.nth-value-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: NTH_VALUE
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   NTH_VALUE() returns the nth value of the measure_expr expression in the searching direction that is defined by [ FROM { FIRST | LAST } ]. The default direction is FROM FIRST. You can specify whether to ignore NULL. The windows are a unified analytic_clause.
--   n must be a positive integer. If n is NULL, this function returns an error. If n is greater than the total number of rows in the window, this function returns NULL.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   NTH_VALUE (measure_expr, n) [ FROM { FIRST | LAST } ] [ { RESPECT | IGNORE } NULLS ] OVER (analytic_clause)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT last_name, FIRST_VALUE(salary) OVER(PARTITION BY job_id) first_s, LAST_VALUE(salary) OVER(PARTITION BY job_id) last_s, NTH_VALUE(salary,2) OVER(PARTITION BY job_id) 2nd_s FROM employees;

