SELECT last_name, RANK() OVER(PARTITION BY job_id ORDER BY salary) rank, DENSE_RANK() OVER(PARTITION BY job_id ORDER BY salary) dense_rank, PERCENT_RANK() OVER(PARTITION BY job_id ORDER BY salary) percent_rank FROM employees;

-- [F01-M0239] PERCENTILE_CONT
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-mysql-mode/3450.percentile_cont-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-mysql-mode/3450.percentile_cont-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: PERCENTILE_CONT
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   This function is used to calculate the exact percentile of a specified column. The function sorts the input column in ascending order and returns the interpolated result based on the given percentile value (percentile).
--   Note
--   This function is supported in OceanBase Database V4.3.5 BP1 and later.
--   SECTION: Limitations and considerations
--   The PERCENTILE_CONT function does not support the DATETIME type in the current version.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   • expr: the target column for which to calculate the percentile. It must be a numeric type. For more information about the supported types, see Supported types.
--   PERCENTILE_CONT automatically ignores NULL values in the input column.
--   • percentile: the specified percentile value, indicating the position of the target value in the data distribution. It is a floating-point constant in the range [0, 1].
--   SECTION: Supported types
--   • Integer types: TINYINT, SMALLINT, MEDIUMINT, INT/INTEGER, and BIGINT.
--   • Fixed-point types: DECIMAL and NUMERIC.
--   • Floating-point types: FLOAT and DOUBLE.
--   • Bit-value type: BIT.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is the same as that of expr.
--   It returns a value at the specified percentile. If no input value is exactly at the required percentile, the result is calculated using linear interpolation between the two closest input values.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   PERCENTILE_CONT(expr, percentile)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT col1, PERCENTILE_CONT(col2, 0.5)
   FROM test_tbl1
   GROUP BY col1;

-- [F01-M0240] RANK
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-mysql-mode/3500.rank-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-mysql-mode/3500.rank-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: RANK
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   The function assigns a rank (ranking) to each row in the query result set based on the column specified in the order_by_clause.
--   The ranks start from 1 in each partition and increment by 1. If two rows have the same rank, the next return value skips the rank. For example, if the rank of two rows is 1, the rank of the next row is 3.
--   • query_partition_clause: specifies the grouping (partitioning) for the window, that is, the rows within each group are ranked.
--   • order_by_clause: specifies the sorting order of rows within each partition.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   RANK() OVER ([query_partition_clause] order_by_clause)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT last_name, RANK() OVER(PARTITION BY job_id ORDER BY salary) RANK FROM employees;

