-- [F01-M0245] BIT_OR
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-mysql-mode/400.bit-or-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-mysql-mode/400.bit-or-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: BIT_OR
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   BIT_OR() returns the bitwise OR of all bits in expr.
--   The result is a binary string or a number. This depends on the type of the argument value. When the argument value contains a binary string and the argument is not a hexadecimal, bit, or NULL literal, a binary string result is calculated. Otherwise, a numeric result is calculated. If necessary, the function converts the argument value to an unsigned 64-bit integer.
--   If no matching row is found, BIT_OR() returns a neutral value, all bits of which are set to 0. The neutral value has the same length as the argument value. NULL has no effect on the result unless all values are NULL. In this case, the result is a neutral value that has the same length as the argument value.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   BIT_OR(expr) OVER (analytic_clause)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM tbl1;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT year,month,BIT_OR(day) OVER (PARTITION BY year, month) AS days FROM tbl1;

