-- [F01-M0289] CURRENT_USER
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/600.information-functions-of-mysql-mode/600.current-user-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/200.common-tenant-of-mysql-mode/400.functions-of-mysql-mode/600.information-functions-of-mysql-mode/600.current-user-of-mysql-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: CURRENT_USER
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   OceanBase Database authenticates the login account by using the username and host name in combination. You can call this function to return the username as a utf8mb4 string.
--   When you log in to OceanBase Database, OceanBase Database matches the username and host name. In the following example, two users are created: testUser@192.168.%.% and testUser@192.%.%.%. They can be queried in the mysql.user table.
--   In a MySQL-compatible tenant, different combinations of usernames and host names are identified as different users. They can be granted different privileges. In the following example, the SELECT and UPDATE privileges are granted to the testUser@192.168.%.% user, and the SELECT privilege is granted to the testUser@192.%.%.% user.
--   Log in to OceanBase Database as the testUser user, and execute CURRENT_USER(). The result is as follows:
--   Delete the testUser@192.168.%.% user, connect to OceanBase Database by using the testUser user, and execute CURRENT_USER(). The result is as follows:
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   CURRENT_USER()
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT user,host FROM mysql.user ORDER BY user,host;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT,UPDATE ON *.* TO testUser@192.168.%.% IDENTIFIED BY '******';
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT ON *.* TO testUser@192.%.%.% IDENTIFIED BY '******';
-- Example 4: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT CURRENT_USER();

