-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: JSON_TYPE
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   JSON_TYPE() returns a utf8mb4 string that indicates the type of a JSON value.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Description
--   The value of json_val can be an object, an array, or a scalar type.
--   If the argument is NULL, NULL is returned. If the argument is not a valid JSON value, an error is returned.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   JSON_TYPE(json_val)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT JSON_TYPE(JSON_EXTRACT(@jn, '$.a'));
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT JSON_TYPE(JSON_EXTRACT(@jn, '$.a[0]'));
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT JSON_TYPE(JSON_EXTRACT(@jn, '$.a[1]'));
-- Example 4: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT JSON_TYPE(NULL);
-- Example 5: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT JSON_TYPE(1);

