-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: Overview of TABLE FUNCTIONS
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   Table functions are used to expand semi-structured data into a relational row set, allowing them to be referenced in SELECT ... FROM statements just like tables.
--   Common entry points for table functions in MySQL-compatible mode include:
--   • JSON_TABLE(...) : Expands a JSON document into rows and columns.
--   • FILES(...) : reads data from an external file and participates in the query as a rowset.
--   SECTION: Syntax features
--   SECTION: Usage
--   Table functions are used as table factors and typically appear in the FROM clause.
--   SECTION: Alias requirements
--   You must specify a table alias for JSON_TABLE.
--   SECTION: Basic syntax of JSON_TABLE
--   SECTION: FILES Syntax
--   SECTION: Column definition capabilities
--   column_list supports the following types:
--   • column_name FOR ORDINALITY
--   • column_name data_type PATH path_literal [ON EMPTY] [ON ERROR]
--   • column_name data_type EXISTS PATH path_literal
--   • NESTED [PATH] path_literal COLUMNS (column_list)
--   SECTION: Functions for returning table schemas
--   Name | Purpose
--   JSON_TABLE | Map JSON documents to relational tables
--   FILES | Generate a queryable rowset from an external file
--   SECTION: References
--   • JSON_TABLE function
--   • FILES table function
--   • SELECT statement
--   • LOAD DATA statement
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   JSON_TABLE(expr, path_literal COLUMNS (column_list)) [AS] alias
-- Documented SQL syntax 2 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   FILES(location_expr, format_expr [, pattern_expr])
