-- [F02-O0034] SYS_CONNECT_BY_PATH
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/1000.hierarchical-functions-of-oracle-mode/100.sys-connect-by-path-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/1000.hierarchical-functions-of-oracle-mode/100.sys-connect-by-path-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: SYS_CONNECT_BY_PATH
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   SYS_CONNECT_BY_PATH() returns the paths of column values from the root to nodes. The column values of each row returned by the CONNECT BY condition are separated with the specified separator.
--   Note
--   This function is valid only in hierarchical queries.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   column | The column whose values need to be returned. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, or NVARCHAR2 data type.
--   char | The separator. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, or NVARCHAR2 data type.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is VARCHAR2.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   SYS_CONNECT_BY_PATH(column,'char')
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM tbl1;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT z_id,name,n_id,SYS_CONNECT_BY_PATH(name, '/') "Path"
          FROM tbl1
          START WITH n_id IS NULL
          CONNECT BY PRIOR n_id = z_id
          ORDER BY z_id;

-- [F02-O0035] EMPTY_BLOB
