-- [F02-O0069] CHR
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/100.chr-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/100.chr-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: CHR
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   CHR() returns a character based on a numeric argument. The return value is associated with the character set of the current system.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   n is the NUMBER code used to retrieve the corresponding character. It is an integer in the range of \[0, 4294967295\].
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return value is associated with the character set of the system. OceanBase Database supports the following character sets: binary, utf8mb4, gbk, utf16, and gb18030.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   CHR(n)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT CHR(25700) AS str FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0070] REGEXP_REPLACE
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1000.regexp-replace-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1000.regexp-replace-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: REGEXP_REPLACE
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   REGEXP_REPLACE() replaces characters in source_char that match a regular expression with characters in replace_string.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   source_char | A character expression that serves as the search value. It is usually a character column It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   pattern | The regular expression. It is usually a text literal and can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, or NVARCHAR2 data type.
--   replace_string | The replacement string. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   position | The position where the function begins searching for a match. This optional parameter is a positive integer. The default value is 1, which means that the function begins searching at the first character of source_char.
--   occurrence | A non-negative integer that specifies the occurrence to be replaced. If you specify 0, OceanBase Database replaces all occurrences of the match. If you specify a positive integer n, OceanBase Database replaces the nth occurrence.
--   match_param | A character expression of the VARCHAR2 or CHAR data type. This parameter allows you to change the default matching behavior of the function. i indicates case-insensitive matching. c indicates case-sensitive matching. n allows the period (.) to match the newline character. m assumes that the source string has multiple lines. x ignores whitespace characters. By default, whitespace characters match each other.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is the same as the data type of source_char.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   REGEXP_REPLACE(source_char, pattern
--                    [, replace_string
--                       [, position
--                         [, occurrence
--                           [, match_param ]
--                         ]
--                       ]
--                    ]
--                  )
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_REPLACE('OCEANBASE database', '(A|B){1}', '*',1,0) AS "REGEXP_REPLACE" FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0071] REGEXP_SUBSTR
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1100.regexp-substr-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1100.regexp-substr-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: REGEXP_SUBSTR
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   This function allows you to search for a string using a regular expression and return the matched string, extending the functionality of the SUBSTR function.
--   It is similar to REGEXP_INSTR, but instead of returning the position of the substring, it returns the substring itself. This function is particularly useful when you only need the content of the matched string and do not require its position in the source string.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   source_char | The character expression to be used as the search value. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   pattern | The regular expression extraction rule. It is typically a text literal and can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, or NVARCHAR2 data type.
--   position | The starting position for the regular expression match. It is a positive integer and is optional. The default value is 1, indicating that the search for source_char starts from the first character.
--   occurrence | The number of the regular expression match in source_char. It is optional. The default value is 1. If occurrence is greater than 1, the search for the second regular expression match starts from the first character after the first match, and so on.
--   match_param | The character expression that specifies the modified regular expression matching mode. It is optional. It can be of the VARCHAR2 or CHAR data type. i indicates case-insensitive matching. c indicates case-sensitive matching. n indicates that the dot . can match a newline character. m indicates multiline mode. x indicates that whitespace characters are ignored. By default, whitespace characters match each other.
--   subexpr | The subexpression in pattern to be returned. It is a non-negative integer from 0 to 9 and is optional. The default value is 0, indicating that the first subexpression is returned.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   Returns a VARCHAR2 or CLOB data type that is the same as the source_char parameter.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   REGEXP_SUBSTR(source_char, pattern
--                  [, position [, occurrence [, match_param [, subexpr] ] ] ]
--                 )
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_SUBSTR('abc,def,ghi', '[^,]+',1,1) AS "First",
                REGEXP_SUBSTR('abc,def,ghi', '[^,]+',1,2) AS "Second",
                REGEXP_SUBSTR('abc,def,ghi', '[^,]+',1,3) AS "Third"
       FROM DUAL;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_SUBSTR('abcdefghi', 'abc(d(e(f)gh)i)',1,1,'i',2) FROM DUAL;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REGEXP_SUBSTR('<div><p>Hello</p><p>World</p></div>', '<p>.*?</p>') FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0072] REPLACE
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1200.replace-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1200.replace-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: REPLACE
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   REPLACE() replaces all occurrences of the substring that matches char2 in char1 with char3.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   char1 | The source string. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   char2 | The substring to be replaced. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   char3 | The replacement string. The default value is NULL rather than a blank space, which means that the substring will be removed. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type. Note: If char3 is not specified or is NULL, all occurrences of char2 in char1 will be removed. If char2 is NULL, char1 is returned.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The string returned is in the same character set as char1.
--   • If char1 is of the LOB data type, the return type is CLOB.
--   • If char1 is not of the LOB data type, the return type is VARCHA2.
-- Documented SQL syntax: no separate SQL syntax block on this source page; see notes and source.
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT REPLACE('Oceanbase','b','B') "replace" FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0073] RPAD
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1300.rpad-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1300.rpad-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: RPAD
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   RPAD() right-pads char1 with char2 to n characters in length.
--   Note
--   If char1 is longer than n characters, the function returns the leftmost n characters of char1.
--   If char1 is shorter than n characters, and the combined length of char1 and char2 is greater than n characters, the function returns the leftmost n characters of the padded string.
--   If char1 is shorter than n characters, and the combined length of char1 and char2 is smaller than n characters, the function returns the leftmost n characters of a string obtained by padding char1 with char2 replicated for multiple times, where the total length of the string is greater than or equal to n characters.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   char1 | The source string. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   n | The total length of the returned string after padding. It must be of the NUMBER data type or any data type that can be implicitly converted to the NUMBER data type. Note: A non-integer value is rounded down to an integer.
--   char2 | The string to be padded with. The default value is a blank space. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   • If char1 is of the character data type, the return type is VARCHAR2.
--   • If char1 is of the national character data type, the return type is NVARCHAR2.
--   • If char1 is of the LOB data type, the return type is LOB.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   RPAD(char1,n[,char2])
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT  RPAD('ABCDE',10,'*') "RPAD" FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0074] RTRIM
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1400.rtrim-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1400.rtrim-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: RTRIM
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   RTRIM() removes, from the right end of char1, all characters contained in char2 until a character not in char2 is reached on the right end of char1 and returns the result.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   char1 | The source string. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   char2 | The set of characters to be removed from the string. This parameter is optional. The default value is a blank space. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   • If char1 is of the CHAR or VARCHAR2 data type, the return type is VARCHAR2.
--   • If char1 is of the NCHAR or NVARCHAR2 data type, the return type is NVARCHAR2.
--   • If char1 is of the LOB data type, the return type is LOB.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   RTRIM(char1 [,char2])
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT RTRIM('OceanBaseX**XX*X','*X') "RTRIM" FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0075] SOUNDEX
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1450.soundex-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1450.soundex-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: SOUNDEX
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   SOUNDEX() returns the phonetic representation of a string. You can use this function to compare English words that have similar pronunciations but different spellings.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   char specifies the string to convert.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is a string.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   SOUNDEX(char)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT SOUNDEX('OceanBase') AS result FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0076] SUBSTR
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1500.substr-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1500.substr-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: SUBSTR
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   SUBSTR() extracts a substring from a string. A multi-byte character, such as a Chinese character or a full-width character, counts as one character.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   char1 | The string from which the substring is to be extracted. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   n1 | The starting position for extraction. It is of the NUMBER data type. Note If n1 is 0, it is treated as 1, which mean that extraction starts at the first character of char1. If n1 is positive, the function counts from the n1-th character of char1 to find the first character of the substring. If n1 is negative, the function counts backward n1 characters from the end of char1 to find the first character of the substring.
--   n2 | The length of the substring to extract. This optional parameter is of the NUMBER data type. Note If you do not specify n2, the function extracts all characters to the end of char1 starting from the n1-th character. If n2 is less than 0, the function returns NULL.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is the same as the data type of char1.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   SUBSTR(char1,n1[,n2])
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT SUBSTR('OceanBase',1,5) "SUBSTR1",SUBSTR('OceanBase',-3,2.1) "SUBSTR2" FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0077] SUBSTRB
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1550.substrb-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1550.substrb-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: SUBSTRB
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   SUBSTRB() extracts a substring from a string in bytes.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   char | The string from which to extract a substring.
--   position | The starting byte position for extraction. A value of 0 is treated as 1. If the value is negative, the starting position is counted backward from the end of the string.
--   substring_length | Optional. The number of bytes to extract. If this parameter is omitted, the function extracts through the end of the string.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is a string.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   SUBSTRB(char, position [, substring_length])
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT SUBSTRB('éOceanBase', 3, 5) AS result FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0078] TRANSLATE
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1600.translate-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1600.translate-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: TRANSLATE
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   TRANSLATE() replaces characters in char1 that match char2 with the corresponding characters in char3.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   char1 | The source character expression or variable.
--   char2 | The set of characters to be replaced in char1.
--   char3 | The set of characters to replace char2.
--   Note
--   char1, char2, and char3 each can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   If char3 contains more characters than char2, the extra characters at the end of char3 are invalid.
--   If char3 contains fewer characters than char2, the extra characters at the end of char2 are removed from the return value.
--   If char3 contains 0 characters, the function returns NULL.
--   If char2 contains duplicate characters, the mapping in char3 corresponding to the first occurrence of the character is used for replacement. For example, if char2 is aab and char3 is 123, the function replaces all a in char1 with 1 and all b with 3.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is the same as the data type of char1.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   TRANSLATE(char1,char2,char3)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT TRANSLATE('OceanBase','ae','AE') "TRANSLATE" FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0079] TRANSLATE ... USING
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1700.translate-using-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1700.translate-using-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: TRANSLATE ... USING
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   TRANSLATE ... USING() converts a string into a specified database character set or national character set.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   char | The string to be converted. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   CHAR_CS | Specifies that the source string is to be converted into the database character set.
--   NCHAR_CS | Specifies that the source string is to be converted into the national character set.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   • If you specify CHAR_CS, the return type is VARCHAR2.
--   • If you specify NCHAR_CS, the return type is NVARCHAR2.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   TRANSLATE(char USING{ CHAR_CS | NCHAR_CS })
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT LENGTHB(TRANSLATE('OceanBase' USING CHAR_CS)) "VARCHAR2",
                 LENGTHB(TRANSLATE('OceanBase' USING NCHAR_CS)) "NVARCHAR2"
          FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0080] TRIM
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1800.trim-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/1800.trim-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: TRIM
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   TRIM() removes leading and/or trailing characters from a string.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   LEADING \ | TRAILING \ | BOTH | The location of the characters to be removed from the string. LEADING indicates the leading characters. If you specify LEADING, the function removes any leading characters that are equal to the value of trim_character. TRAILING indicates the trailing characters. If you specify TRAILING, the function removes any trailing characters that are equal to the value of trim_character. BOTH indicates both the leading and trailing characters. If you specify BOTH or none of the three options, the function removes the leading and trailing characters that are equal to the value of trim_character.
--   trim_character | The character to be removed. Only a single character can be specified for trim_character. If you do not specify trim_character, the default value is a blank space.
--   trim_source | The string that you want to trim. If you specify only trim_source, the function removes the leading and trailing blank spaces.
--   Note
--   trim_character and trim_source can be VARCHAR2 or any data type that can be implicitly converted to VARCHAR2. If the function returns a value of the VARCHAR2 data type, the maximum length of the value is the length of the value of trim_source.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   • If trim_source is of the CHAR or VARCHAR2 data type, the return type is VARCHAR2.
--   • If trim_source is of the NCHAR or NVARCHAR2 data type, the return type is NVARCHAR2.
--   • If trim_source is of the CLOB data type, the return type is CLOB.
--   • If trim_source or trim_character is NULL, the TRIM() function returns NULL.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   TRIM([{{ LEADING | TRAILING | BOTH }[ trim_character ]|trim_character }FROM] trim_source)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT TRIM('X' FROM 'XXOceanBaseXX') "TRIM" FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0081] UPPER
-- Mode: Oracle
