-- [F02-O0087] LPAD
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/500.lpad-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/500.lpad-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: LPAD
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   LPAD() left-pads char1 with char2 to n characters in length.
--   Note
--   If char1 is longer than n characters, the function returns the leftmost n characters of char1.
--   If char1 is shorter than n characters, and the combined length of char1 and char2 is greater than n characters, the function returns the rightmost n characters of the padded string.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   char1 | The source string. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   n | The total length of the returned string after padding. It must be of the NUMBER data type or any data type that can be implicitly converted to the NUMBER data type. Note: A non-integer value is rounded down to an integer.
--   char2 | The string to be padded with. The default value is a blank space. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   • If char1 is of the character data type, the return type is VARCHAR2.
--   • If char1 is of the national character data type, the return type is NVARCHAR2.
--   • If char1 is of the LOB data type, the return type is LOB.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   LPAD(char1,n[,char2])
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT  LPAD('ABCDE',10,'*') FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0088] LTRIM
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/600.ltrim-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/600.ltrim-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: LTRIM
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   LTRIM() removes, from the left end of char1, all characters contained in char2 until a character not in char2 is reached on the left end of char1 and returns the result.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   char1 | The source string. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   char2 | The set of characters to be removed from the string. If you do not specify char2, the default value is a blank space. It can be of the CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, NVARCHAR2, or CLOB data type.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   • If char1 is of the CHAR or VARCHAR2 data type, the return type is VARCHAR2.
--   • If char1 is of the NCHAR or NVARCHAR2 data type, the return type is NVARCHAR2.
--   • If char1 is of the LOB data type, the return type is LOB.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   LTRIM(char1 [,char2])
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT LTRIM('#a￥!aa￥  Hello World!','#a￥! ') text FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0089] MAX_PT
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/650.max-pt-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/650.max-pt-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: MAX_PT
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   This function returns the maximum value of the partitioning key among the partitions that contain data in a partitioned table.
--   Note
--   This function is supported starting from OceanBase Database V4.4.2 BP3.
--   SECTION: Limitations and considerations
--   • The caller must have SELECT object privileges on the target table (Oracle-compatible mode checks object-level privileges). Otherwise, the call fails.
--   • Table names in parameters must be constants (string literals that can be determined during parsing). Variables or non-constant expressions are not supported as table name parameters.
--   • The table to be queried must be a partitioned table, and the partition type must be RANGE or LIST.
--   • The table to be queried must be an internal table. External tables and DBLink are not supported.
--   • Only a single-column partitioning key is supported; a multi-column partitioning key is not supported.
--   • Expression partitioning is not supported. If the partitioning key is not a direct reference to a single column but an expression in the partition definition, MAX_PT cannot be used.
--   • For a subpartitioned table, only the values of the first-level partitions are returned.
--   • If all partitions are empty, NULL is returned.
--   • If different partition values exist within the same partition, the result of MAX_PT is undefined, and a value from any row in that partition will be returned.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Notice
--   In Oracle-compatible mode, schema names and table names are case-sensitive and must match the names stored in the data dictionary. Objects created without double quotes are typically stored in uppercase in the dictionary; identifiers created with double quotes are stored in the case used when they were created, and the string must match exactly when invoking them.
--   • schema_name: Optional. The name of the schema that contains the table. If not specified, the table is in the default schema of the current session. You can also use the 'schema_name.table_name' format in the parameter string (separated by at most one .).
--   • table_name: The name of the table.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   Returns the maximum partition value (compared by using the max() logic) among the partitions that contain data. The data type is the same as the partitioning key type.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   MAX_PT('[schema_name.]table_name')
-- Official SELECT example: none available on this source page. Use the preserved signature in a SELECT statement.

