-- [F02-O0091] NLSSORT
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/800.nlssort-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.string-functions-that-return-a-string-of-oracle-mode/800.nlssort-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: NLSSORT
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   This function returns the sort key value (Sortkey) of a character based on the specified collation, which can be specified explicitly or implicitly.
--   The NLSSORT() function generates a sort key value based on the specified collation. A sort key is a byte string that is used to sort characters based on the specified collation.
--   The length of the sort key is affected by the MAX_STRING_SIZE parameter. If MAX_STRING_SIZE=EXTENDED, the maximum length of the return value is 32767 bytes. If the sort key exceeds this limit, the function will fail and return an error message "unable to create the collation key".
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   char | This parameter supports literals and expressions (including column names). The data type can be CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, or NVARCHAR2. If the input data type is CLOB, it will be implicitly converted.
--   nlsparam | Specifies the collation. The fixed format of nlsparam is NLS_SORT = collation. collation specifies the language collation name. OceanBase Database V4.2 and later versions support the GB18030_2022 character set for Chinese sorting. For the Chinese character set, the following three Chinese collations are supported: SCHINESE_PINYIN2_M: Sorts characters by pinyin. SCHINESE_STROKE2_M: Sorts characters by stroke count. SCHINESE_RADICAL2_M: Sorts characters by radical. For Unicode, the following three Chinese collations are supported: UCA0900_SCHINESE_PINYIN: Sorts characters by pinyin. UCA0900_SCHINESE_STROKE: Sorts characters by stroke count UCA0900_SCHINESE_RADICAL: First sorts characters by radical, then by stroke count. If nlsparam is omitted, the function uses the default collation of the character set.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   NLSSORT(char [, 'nlsparam' ])
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM tbl1;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM tbl1 ORDER BY NLSSORT(name,'NLS_SORT = SCHINESE_PINYIN2_M');

