-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: SYSTIMESTAMP
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   SYSTIMESTAMP returns the current system date and time. The return value includes the time zone and the fractional seconds with 6 decimal places.
--   Notice
--   This function gets the date and time in the time zone of the operating system on which the OBServer node resides, regardless of the time zone of the current session.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   SYSTIMESTAMP
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT SYSTIMESTAMP FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0112] TO_CHAR(datetime)
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/400.date-and-time-functions-of-oracle-mode/1800.to-char-datetime-oracle-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/400.date-and-time-functions-of-oracle-mode/1800.to-char-datetime-oracle-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: TO_CHAR(datetime)
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   TO_CHAR(datetime) converts a datetime or interval value of the DATE, TIMESTAMP, TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE, TIMESTAMP WITH LOCAL TIME ZONE, INTERVAL DAY TO SECOND, or INTERVAL YEAR TO MONTH data type to a value of the VARCHAR2 data type in the format specified by the fmt parameter.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   datetime \ | interval | The type of the value to be converted. datetime indicates a datetime value of the DATE, TIMESTAMP, TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE, TIMESTAMP WITH LOCAL TIME ZONE, INTERVAL DAY TO SECOND, or INTERVAL YEAR TO MONTH data type. interval indicates an interval value.
--   fmt | The format of the return value. This parameter is optional. If you do not specify fmt, the value of datetime is converted to a value of the VARCHAR2 data type in the following format: A value of the DATE, TIMESTAMP, TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE, or TIMESTAMP WITH LOCAL TIME ZONE data type is converted to a value in the default datetime format of OceanBase Database. For the default formats of different datetime types, see Datetime and interval data types. A value of the INTERVAL DAY TO SECOND or INTERVAL YEAR TO MONTH data type is converted to an interval value in the numeric format.
--   nlsparam | The language in which month and day names are returned.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is VARCHAR2.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   TO_CHAR({ datetime | interval } [, fmt [, 'nlsparam' ] ])
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT TO_CHAR(SYSDATE,'yyyy/mm/dd'),TO_CHAR(SYSDATE,'DS DL') FROM DUAL;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT TO_CHAR(interval'1' year, 'SS-MI-HH', 'nls_language = AMERICAN') FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0113] TO_DSINTERVAL
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/400.date-and-time-functions-of-oracle-mode/1900.to-dsinterval-date-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/200.single-row-functions-of-oracle-mode/400.date-and-time-functions-of-oracle-mode/1900.to-dsinterval-date-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: TO_DSINTERVAL
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   This function converts a string of CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, or NVARCHAR2 data type to an INTERVAL DAY TO SECOND value, which can be used to add or subtract a date and time value.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   \[+ \ | -\]days hours:minutes:seconds\[.frac_secs\] | A string of CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, or NVARCHAR2 data type that conforms to the parameter format. days specifies the number of days, which is an integer in the range of \[0,999999999\]. hours specifies the number of hours, which is an integer in the range of \[0,23\]. minutes specifies the number of minutes, which is an integer in the range of \[0,59\]. seconds specifies the number of seconds, which is an integer in the range of \[0,59\].
--   \[-\] P\[days D\] \[T\[hours H\]\[minutes M\]\[seconds\[.frac_secs\]S\] | A string of CHAR, VARCHAR2, NCHAR, or NVARCHAR2 data type that conforms to the parameter format. Notice The value cannot contain spaces.
--   frac_secs | The decimal part of seconds, which is an integer in the range of \[0,999999999\].
--   SECTION: Return type
--   INTERVAL DAY TO SECOND
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   /*SQL date format*/
--   TO_DSINTERVAL ('[+ | -] days hours:minutes:seconds[.frac_secs]')
--
--   /*ISO date format*/
--   TO_DSINTERVAL ('[-] P[days D]
--   [T[hours H][minutes M][seconds[.frac_secs]S]]')
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT TO_CHAR(TIMESTAMP '2021-11-11 00:00:00'+TO_DSINTERVAL('100 00:00:00'),
       'YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS') "Time Stamp" FROM DUAL;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT TO_CHAR(TIMESTAMP '2021-11-11 00:00:00'+TO_DSINTERVAL('P100DT5H'),
      'YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS') "Time Stamp" FROM DUAL;

