-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/1700.min-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: MIN
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   MIN() returns the minimum value in the specified column. You can use this function as an aggregate or analytic function.
--   Note
--   When you use this function as an analytic function, use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. The function operates on a group of rows to return a list of values.
--   When you use this function as an aggregate function, the function operates on a set of rows and returns a single value. You do not need to add the OVER clause.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   DISTINCT \ | UNIQUE \ | ALL | Optional. Specifies whether to remove duplicates during the query. Default value: ALL. ALL: returns all values, including duplicate rows. Rows with NULLs are ignored. DISTINCT: returns deduplicated rows. Rows with NULLs are ignored. UNIQUE: returns deduplicated rows. Rows with NULLs are ignored.
--   expr | An expression or column of the numeric, character, date, or other data types.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is the same as the data type of expr.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   MIN([ DISTINCT | UNIQUE | ALL ] expr) [ OVER (analytic_clause) ]
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM employees;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT MIN(salary)  FROM employees;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT department_id,last_name,hiredate,salary,
                 MIN(salary) OVER(PARTITION BY department_id) AS p_cmin
          FROM employees;

-- [F02-O0219] NTILE
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/1800.ntile-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/1800.ntile-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: NTILE
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   NTILE() is an analytic function. It divides an ordered dataset into several groups and assigns an appropriate group number to each row. The group number is in the range from 1 to expr.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   expr | The desired number of groups. The value of expr must evaluate to a positive number greater than or equal to 1. If expr is a non-integer constant, the database will truncate the value to an integer. If expr is greater than the number of rows, each group is filled in with one row only, and the other groups remain empty. Notice: Do not nest an analytic function in NTILE() or any other analytic function. However, you can use other built-in function expressions in expr.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is NUMERIC.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   NTILE (expr) OVER ([ query_partition_clause ] order_by_clause)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM emp_msg;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT ename,sal,NTILE(5) OVER (ORDER BY sal DESC) sal_ntile FROM emp_msg ORDER BY sal DESC;

-- [F02-O0220] NTH_VALUE
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/1900.nth-value-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/1900.nth-value-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: NTH_VALUE
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   NTH_VALUE() returns the measure_expr value in the nth row in the window defined in analytic_clause.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   measure_expr | The field of the data to return.
--   n | The nth row of the measured values to return. n is a positive integer. Notice If n is NULL, the function returns an error. If n is greater than the total number of rows in the window, the function returns NULL.
--   FROM { FIRST \ | LAST } | The calculation direction. This parameter is optional. Default value: FROM FIRST. FROM FIRST indicates that the calculation starts from the first row in the window. FROM LAST indicates that the calculation starts from the last row in the window.
--   {RESPECT \ | IGNORE} NULLS | Optional. Specifies whether to ignore NULLs. Default value: RESPECT NULLS. RESPECT NULLS specifies to include NULLs in the calculation. IGNORE NULLS specifies to ignore NULLs in the calculation.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is the same as the data type of the measure_expr parameter.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   NTH_VALUE (measure_expr, n)
--     [ FROM { FIRST | LAST } ][ { RESPECT | IGNORE } NULLS ]
--   OVER (analytic_clause)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM emp_msg;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT deptno,ename,sal,
               NTH_VALUE(sal,4) OVER (PARTITION BY deptno ORDER BY sal DESC
               ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING) AS third_most_sal
      FROM emp_msg
      ORDER BY deptno,sal DESC;

-- [F02-O0221] AVG
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.avg-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/200.avg-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: AVG
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   AVG() returns the average of input expression values of a numeric data type or a data type that can be converted to a numeric data type. You can use this function as an aggregate or analytic function.
--   Note
--   When you use this function as an analytic function, use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. The function operates on a group of rows to return a list of values.
--   When you use this function as an aggregate function, the function operates on a set of rows and returns a single value. You do not need to add the OVER clause.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   DISTINCT \ | UNIQUE \ | ALL | Specifies whether to remove duplicates during the execution of the query. This parameter is optional. Default value: ALL. ALL: indicates to accept duplicate values and ignore NULLs in the data. DISTINCT: indicates to remove duplicates and ignore NULLs in the data. UNIQUE: indicates to remove duplicates and ignore NULLs in the data.
--   expr | The name of the column to be calculated. The column is of a numeric data type or a data type that can be converted to a numeric data type. Supported numeric data types are NUMBER, FLOAT, BINARY_FLOAT, and BINARY_DOUBLE.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   Notice
--   If you specify the DISTINCT or UNIQUE keyword, you can specify only analytic_clause. order_by_clause and windowing_clause are not allowed.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is the same as the data type of expr.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   AVG([ DISTINCT | UNIQUE | ALL ] expr) [ OVER (analytic_clause) ]
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM tbl1;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT AVG(col3) FROM tbl1;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT col1,col2,col3, AVG(col3)
              OVER (PARTITION BY col1 ORDER BY col2 ROWS BETWEEN 1 PRECEDING AND 1 FOLLOWING) AS over_col2
          FROM tbl1
          ORDER BY col1,col2,col3;

-- [F02-O0222] PERCENT_RANK
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2000.percent-rank-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2000.percent-rank-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: PERCENT_RANK
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   This function calculates the cumulative distribution of a value in a set of values. It is similar to the CUME_DIST function. You can use this function as an aggregate or analytic function.
--   • As an aggregate function, PERCENT_RANK calculates the position percentage of a value in a set. The return value is in the range (0, 1\]. If there are N rows of data, and the value of expr is greater than the value of the second row and less than the value of the third row, the position percentage is 2/N. The number of constant parameter expressions must be the same as the number of expressions in the ORDER BY clause of the aggregate function, and their types must be compatible. If a specified value is repeated in the grouping sequence, the two identical values are treated as one value.
--   • As an analytic function, PERCENT_RANK calculates the percentage ranking of each row in a column or combination of columns. The return value is in the range \[0, 1\]. When there are duplicate ranking values, the same ranking is assigned to the rows with the same values, and the number of rows with the same value is recorded in the next ranking. The PERCENT_RANK function for the first row in any set is 0. The position percentage is calculated as: position percentage = rank / maximum rank. For example, see the following table:
--   N | Rank | Position Percentage
--   A | 0 | 0
--   B | 1 | 0.25
--   C | 2 | 0.5
--   D | 3 | 0.75
--   E | 4 | 1
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   expr | Specifies the value to query.
--   expr_col | Specifies the column name corresponding to the value to query.
--   DESC \ | ASC | Specifies the sorting order. This is an optional parameter. ASC specifies ascending order, which is the default. DESC specifies descending order.
--   NULLS { FIRST \ | LAST } | Specifies the position of NULL values in expr_col after sorting. This is an optional parameter. NULLS FIRST specifies that NULL values are placed before non-null values. NULLS LAST specifies that NULL values are placed after non-null values, which is the default.
--   OVER | Specifies the window for calculation using the OVER clause. For more information, see Analytic Function Description.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   NUMBER
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   /*Aggregate syntax*/
--   PERCENT_RANK(expr [, expr ...]) WITHIN GROUP
--                  ( ORDER BY expr_col [ DESC | ASC ][ NULLS { FIRST | LAST } ]
--                             [,expr_col [ DESC | ASC ][ NULLS { FIRST | LAST } ]]...
--                  )
--
--   /*Analytic syntax*/
--   PERCENT_RANK( ) OVER ([query_partition_clause] order_by_clause)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM emp_msg;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT PERCENT_RANK(3300) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY sal) FROM emp_msg;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT deptno,ename,sal,
              PERCENT_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY deptno ORDER BY sal DESC) AS pr1
          FROM emp_msg;

-- [F02-O0223] PERCENTILE_CONT
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2100.percentile-cont-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2100.percentile-cont-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: PERCENTILE_CONT
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   This function is the inverse of the cumulative distribution function for a continuous distribution model. It returns an interpolated value at the specified percentile for the given ordering. Null values are ignored in the computation.
--   Note
--   When used as an analytic function, you must use the OVER clause to define the window for computation. It computes over a set of rows and returns multiple values.
--   When used as an aggregate function, it computes over a set of rows and returns a single value. In this case, the OVER clause is not required.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   percentile | A constant of numeric data type specifying the percentile value. The value must be in the range \[0,1\]. Note The MEDIAN function is equivalent to setting percentile to 0.5.
--   expr | An expression specifying the ordering criteria. The data type must be numeric or datetime. Note expr must be a single expression involving column references. Multiple expressions are not allowed.
--   DESC \ | ASC | Optional. Specifies the sort order. ASC specifies ascending order, which is the default. DESC specifies descending order.
--   OVER | Use the OVER clause to define the window for computation. For more information, see Analytic Function Description.
--   After sorting the rows based on the ordering criteria, the function calculates the row number using the specified percentile (P) and the count of non-null rows (N) with the formula RN = (1+ (P*(N-1)). The final result is computed by linear interpolation between the values at row numbers CRN = CEILING(RN) and FRN = FLOOR(RN). The final result will be as follows:
--   If (CRN = FRN = RN), the result is (value of expression from row at RN); otherwise, the result is:
--   (CRN - RN) (value of expression for row at FRN) + (RN - FRN) (value of expression for row at CRN).
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The same data type as the expr parameter.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   PERCENTILE_CONT(percentile) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY expr [ DESC | ASC ])
--   [ OVER (query_partition_clause) ]
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM tbl1;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT PERCENTILE_CONT(0.1) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY col4) FROM tbl1;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT col1,col4,PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY col4) OVER (PARTITION BY col1)
          FROM tbl1;

-- [F02-O0224] PERCENTILE_DISC
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2200.percentile-disc-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2200.percentile-disc-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: PERCENTILE_DISC
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   PERCENTILE_DISC() returns the value of the smallest cumulative distribution (relative to the sorting specification) that is greater than or equal to the percentile value, based on a percentile value and a specified ordering specification. NULLs are ignored in the calculation.
--   Note
--   When you use this function as an analytic function, use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. The function operates on a group of rows to return a list of values.
--   When you use this function as an aggregate function, the function operates on a set of rows and returns a single value. You do not need to add the OVER clause.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   expr1 | The percentile value. It is a numeric constant in the range of \[0, 1\].
--   expr2 | An expression for sorting and percentile calculation. The data type is numeric or datetime.
--   DESC \ | ASC | Optional. The sorting method of the list. ASC specifies to sort in ascending order. It is the default value. DESC specifies to sort in descending order.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is the same as the data type of expr2.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   PERCENTILE_DISC(expr1) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY expr2 [ DESC | ASC ])
--   [ OVER (query_partition_clause) ]
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM tbl1;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT PERCENTILE_DISC(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY col4) "PERCENTILE_DISC" FROM tbl1;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT col1,col4,PERCENTILE_DISC(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY col4) OVER(PARTITION BY col1) "PERCENTILE_DISC"
          FROM tbl1;

-- [F02-O0225] RANK
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2300.rank-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2300.rank-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: RANK
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   This function calculates the rank of a value within a set of values. When there are duplicate values in the ranking, the same rank is assigned, and the number of rows with the same value is recorded in the next rank. You can use this function as an aggregate or analytic function.
--   Note
--   As an aggregate function, RANK calculates the rank of the assumed rows identified by the parameter expr relative to the specified sorting specification. The parameter expressions expr and the expressions in the ORDER BY clause of the aggregate function must match by position. Therefore, the number of parameters must be the same, and their data types must be compatible.
--   As an analytic function, RANK calculates the rank of each row returned by the query relative to other rows returned by the query, based on the values specified in value_exprs and order_by_clause.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   expr | Specifies the value to query.
--   expr_col | Specifies the column name corresponding to the value to query.
--   DESC \ | ASC | Specifies the sorting method, which is optional. ASC specifies ascending order, which is the default. DESC specifies descending order.
--   NULLS { FIRST \ | LAST } | Specifies the position of NULL values in expr_col after sorting, which is optional. NULLS FIRST specifies that NULL values are placed before non-null values. NULLS LAST specifies that NULL values are placed after non-null values, which is the default.
--   OVER | Defines the window for calculation using the OVER clause. For more information, see Analytic Function Description.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   Returns the NUMBER data type.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   /*Aggregate syntax*/
--   RANK(expr [, expr ]...) WITHIN GROUP
--     ( ORDER BY
--               expr_col [ DESC | ASC ][ NULLS { FIRST | LAST } ]
--               [, expr_col [ DESC | ASC ][ NULLS { FIRST | LAST } ]]...
--     )
--
--   /*Analytic syntax*/
--   RANK() OVER ([ query_partition_clause ] order_by_clause)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM emp_msg;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT RANK(3300) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY sal) FROM emp_msg;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT deptno,ename,sal,
                   RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY deptno ORDER BY sal DESC) "RANK"
          FROM emp_msg;

-- [F02-O0226] RATIO_TO_REPORT
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2400.ratio-to-report-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2400.ratio-to-report-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: RATIO_TO_REPORT
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   RATIO_TO_REPORT() is an analytic function. It returns the ratio between a value and a group of values.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   expr | The name of the field to be queried. Notice: Do not nest expr in a RATIO_TO_REPORT() function or another analytic function.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   If expr is NULL, this function returns NULL. Otherwise, this function returns numeric data.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   RATIO_TO_REPORT(expr) OVER ([query_partition_clause])
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM emp_msg;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT deptno,ename,mgr,RATIO_TO_REPORT(mgr) OVER (PARTITION BY deptno)
      FROM emp_msg;

