-- [F02-O0230] ROW_NUMBER
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2600.row-number-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2600.row-number-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: ROW_NUMBER
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   ROW_NUMBER() is an analytic function. It returns a unique sequence number for each row (group) record. The sequence numbers are sorted by the column specified in order_by_clause, starting from 1. When the same data records are found, the data records are sorted in ascending order based on the sequence of records in the recordset.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   This function returns values of a numeric data type.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   ROW_NUMBER( ) OVER ([ query_partition_clause ] order_by_clause)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM emp_msg;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT deptno,ename,sal,
                 ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY deptno ORDER BY sal DESC)
          FROM emp_msg;

-- [F02-O0231] STDDEV
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2700.stddev-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2700.stddev-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: STDDEV
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   STDDEV() returns the standard deviation of a set of numeric values. The difference between STDDEV() and STDDEV_SAMP() is that the STDDEV() function returns 0 for only one row of input data, whereas STDDEV_SAMP() returns NULL. The standard deviation value in the OceanBase Database is the square root of the variance returned by the VARIANCE() function. You can use this function as an aggregate or analytic function.
--   Note
--   When you use this function as an analytic function, use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. The function operates on a group of rows to return a list of values.
--   When you use this function as an aggregate function, the function operates on a set of rows and returns a single value. You do not need to add the OVER clause.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   DISTINCT \ | UNIQUE \ | ALL | Specifies whether to remove duplicates during the execution of the query. This parameter is optional. Default value: ALL. ALL: All values in the column are included in calculation. DISTINCT: A deduplication keyword, which specifies to include only unique values in the calculation. UNIQUE: A deduplication keyword, which specifies to include only unique values in the calculation.
--   expr | An expression of a numeric data type or a data type that can be converted to a numeric data type.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   Notice
--   If you specify the DISTINCT or UNIQUE keyword, you can specify only analytic_clause. order_by_clause and windowing_clause are not allowed.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is NUMBER.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   STDDEV([ DISTINCT | UNIQUE | ALL ] expr) [ OVER (analytic_clause) ]
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM employees;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT STDDEV(salary) FROM employees;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT last_name,hiredate,salary,STDDEV(salary) OVER (ORDER BY hiredate) "StdDev"
          FROM employees;

-- [F02-O0232] STDDEV_POP
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2800.stddev-pop-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2800.stddev-pop-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: STDDEV_POP
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   STDDEV_POP() returns the population standard deviation of a set of numeric values. A population standard deviation is the arithmetic square root of the population variance. You can use this function as an aggregate or analytic function.
--   Note
--   When you use this function as an analytic function, use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. The function operates on a group of rows to return a list of values.
--   When you use this function as an aggregate function, the function operates on a set of rows and returns a single value. You do not need to add the OVER clause.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   ALL | Optional. Indicates that the function operates on all values in the column. Default value: ALL.
--   expr | An expression of a numeric data type (NUMBER, FLOAT, BINARY_FLOAT, or BINARY_DOUBLE) or a data type that can be converted to a numeric data type.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is the same as the data type of expr.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   STDDEV_POP( [ALL] expr) [ OVER (analytic_clause) ]
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM employees;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT STDDEV_POP(salary) FROM employees;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT department_id,last_name,salary,STDDEV_POP(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY department_id) AS pop_std
          FROM employees
          ORDER BY department_id,last_name,salary;

-- [F02-O0233] STDDEV_SAMP
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2900.stddev-samp-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/2900.stddev-samp-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: STDDEV_SAMP
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   STDDEV_SAMP() returns the sample standard deviation of a set of numeric values. The sample standard deviation is the arithmetic square root of the sample variance. The difference between STDDEV() and STDDEV_SAMP() is that the STDDEV() function returns 0 for only one row of input data, whereas STDDEV_SAMP() returns NULL. You can use this function as an aggregate or analytic function.
--   Note
--   When you use this function as an analytic function, use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. The function operates on a group of rows to return a list of values.
--   When you use this function as an aggregate function, the function operates on a set of rows and returns a single value. You do not need to add the OVER clause.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   ALL | The entire column. This parameter is optional. Default value: ALL.
--   expr | An expression of a numeric data type (NUMBER, FLOAT, BINARY_FLOAT, or BINARY_DOUBLE) or a data type that can be converted to a numeric data type.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is the same as the data type of expr.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   STDDEV_SAMP([ALL] expr) [ OVER (analytic_clause) ]
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM employees;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT STDDEV_SAMP(salary) FROM employees;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT department_id, last_name, hiredate, salary,
                 STDDEV_SAMP(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY department_id
                  ORDER BY hiredate ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS s_samp
          FROM employees;

-- [F02-O0234] CORR
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/300.corr-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/300.corr-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: CORR
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   CORR() returns the correlation coefficient of a set of numeric values. The correlation coefficient measures the strength of correlation between number pairs. The value range is \[-1, 1\]. The value 0 indicates that the number pairs are unrelated. A value less than 0 indicates negative correlation. A value greater than 0 indicates positive correlation. You can use this function as an aggregate or analytic function.
--   Note
--   When you use this function as an analytic function, use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. The function operates on a group of rows to return a list of values.
--   When you use this function as an aggregate function, the function operates on a set of rows and returns a single value. You do not need to add the OVER clause.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   expr1 | The first argument, which is of a numeric data type or a data type that can be implicitly converted to a numeric data type.
--   expr2 | The second argument, which is of a numeric data type or a data type that can be implicitly converted to a numeric data type.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   The database determines the argument with the highest numeric precedence, implicitly converts the remaining arguments to this data type, and returns a result of this data type.
--   The locations of expr1 and expr2 have no influence over the return result.
--   The return result is calculated by using the following formula: COVAR_POP(expr1, expr2) / (STDDEV_POP(expr1) * STDDEV_POP(expr2)).
--   SECTION: Return type
--   If any argument is NULL or you have only one row of data, NULL is returned. Otherwise, a value of the NUMBER type is returned.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   CORR(expr1, expr2) [ OVER (analytic_clause) ]
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM tbl1;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT CORR(col3,col4) FROM tbl1;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT col1,col3,col4,CORR(col3,col4) OVER(PARTITION BY col1) "corr" FROM tbl1;

-- [F02-O0235] SUM
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/3000.sum-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/3000.sum-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: SUM
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   SUM() returns the sum for the specified arguments. You can use this function as an aggregate or analytic function.
--   Note
--   When you use this function as an analytic function, use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. The function operates on a group of rows to return a list of values.
--   When you use this function as an aggregate function, the function operates on a set of rows and returns a single value. You do not need to add the OVER clause.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   DISTINCT \ | UNQIUE \ | ALL | Specifies whether deduplication is required in the calculation. This parameter is optional. Default value: ALL. ALL: All values, including duplicate rows, are taken into computation. Rows with NULLs are ignored. DISTINCT: Duplicate rows are removed. Rows with NULLs are ignored. UNIQUE: Duplicate rows are removed. Rows with NULLs are ignored.
--   expr | An expression of a numeric data type or a data type that can be implicitly converted to a numeric data type.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   Notice
--   If you specify the DISTINCT or UNIQUE keyword, you can only specify the analytic_clause. The order_by_clause and windowing_clause parameters are not allowed.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is the same as the data type of expr.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   SUM([ DISTINCT | UNQIUE | ALL ] expr) [ OVER (analytic_clause) ]
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM employees;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT SUM(salary) FROM employees;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT department_id, last_name, salary,
              SUM(salary) OVER
                  (PARTITION BY department_id ORDER BY salary RANGE UNBOUNDED PRECEDING) s_sum
    FROM employees
    ORDER BY department_id,s_sum;

-- [F02-O0236] VAR_POP
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/3100.var-pop-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/3100.var-pop-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: VAR_POP
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   VAR_POP() returns the population variance for a group of values (with NULLs ignored). You can use this function as an aggregate or analytic function.
--   Note
--   When you use this function as an analytic function, use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. The function operates on a group of rows to return a list of values.
--   When you use this function as an aggregate function, the function operates on a set of rows and returns a single value. You do not need to add the OVER clause.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   expr | The numeric expression that participates in the calculation. It is of a numeric data type or a data type that can be implicitly converted to a numeric data type.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   If this function is applied to an empty set, NULL is returned. The returned result is calculated by using the following formula: SUM((expr - (SUM(expr) / COUNT(expr)))²)/ COUNT(expr).
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is the same as the data type of expr, or this function returns NULL.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   VAR_POP(expr) [ OVER (analytic_clause) ]
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM tbl1;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT VAR_POP(col4) FROM tbl1;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT col1,col2,col4,VAR_POP(col4) OVER(ORDER BY col2) "VAR_POP" FROM tbl1;

