-- [F02-O0239] WMSYS.WM_CONCAT/WM_CONCAT
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/3400.wmsys-wm-concat-and-wm-concat-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/3400.wmsys-wm-concat-and-wm-concat-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: WMSYS.WM_CONCAT/WM_CONCAT
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   WMSYS.WM_CONCAT/WM_CONCAT() aggregates data from a number of measure columns into a single row. You can use this function as an aggregate or analytic function.
--   Note
--   When you use this function as an analytic function, use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. This function divides the query result set into groups based on one or more expressions in query_partition_clause.
--   When you use it as an aggregate function, you do not need to add the OVER keyword.
--   As a single-set aggregate function, the WM_CONCAT() function operates on all rows and returns a single output row.
--   As a group-set aggregate function, the WM_CONCAT() function operates on each group specified in the GROUP BY clause and returns an output row.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   DISTINCT | If you specify the DISTINCT operator, duplicates are eliminated from the result set. This parameter is optional.
--   measure_expr | An expression of any type. NULLs in the measure column are ignored.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is CLOB.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   WM_CONCAT ([DISTINCT] measure_expr) [OVER query_partition_clause]
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM employees;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT WM_CONCAT(last_name) "Emp_list" FROM employees;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT department_id,hiredate,last_name,
              WM_CONCAT(last_name) OVER (PARTITION BY department_id) as "Emp_list"
    FROM employees
    ORDER BY 1,2;

-- [F02-O0240] COUNT
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/400.count-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/400.count-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: COUNT
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   COUNT() returns the number of rows of expr. You can use this function as an aggregate or analytic function.
--   Note
--   When you use this function as an analytic function, use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. The function operates on a group of rows to return a list of values.
--   When you use this function as an aggregate function, the function operates on a set of rows and returns a single value. You do not need to add the OVER clause.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   \* | Specifies to return all rows that meet the condition, including duplicate and NULL rows.
--   DISTINCT \ | UNIQUE \ | ALL | Specifies whether to remove duplicates during the execution of the query. This parameter is optional. Default value: ALL. ALL: returns all values, including duplicate rows. Rows with NULLs are ignored. DISTINCT: returns deduplicated rows. Rows with NULLs are ignored. UNIQUE: returns deduplicated rows. Rows with NULLs are ignored.
--   expr | The name of the column to be calculated.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   Notice
--   The COUNT() function never returns NULL. If you specify expr, the function returns the number of rows where expr is not NULL. If you specify the asterisk (), that is, COUNT(), the function returns the number of all rows. When the DISTINCT, UNIQUE, or ALL parameter is used, separate them from expr with a space.
--   If you specify the DISTINCT or UNIQUE keyword, you can specify only analytic_clause. order_by_clause and windowing_clause are not allowed.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   This function returns a natural number.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   COUNT({ * | [ DISTINCT | UNIQUE | ALL ] expr }) [ OVER (analytic_clause) ]
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM tbl1;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT COUNT(col3),COUNT(*) FROM tbl1;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT col1,col2,
                 COUNT(*) OVER (ORDER BY col3 RANGE BETWEEN 1 PRECEDING AND 3 FOLLOWING) AS mov_count
          FROM tbl1;

-- [F02-O0241] COVAR_POP
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/500.covar-pop-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/500.covar-pop-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: COVAR_POP
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   COVAR_POP() returns the population covariance of a set of number pairs. You can use this function as an aggregate or analytic function.
--   Note
--   When you use this function as an analytic function, use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. The function operates on a group of rows to return a list of values.
--   When you use this function as an aggregate function, the function operates on a set of rows and returns a single value. You do not need to add the OVER clause.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   expr1 | The first expression, which is of a numeric data type or a data type that can be implicitly converted to a numeric data type.
--   expr2 | The second expression, which is of a numeric data type or a data type that can be implicitly converted to a numeric data type.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   The database determines the argument with the highest numeric precedence, implicitly converts the remaining arguments to this data type, and returns a result of this data type.
--   The positions of expr1 and expr2 have no influence over the return result, that is, COVAR_POP(expr1, expr2) is equivalent to COVAR_POP(expr2, expr1).
--   The returned result is calculated by using the following formula: (SUM(expr1 expr2) - SUM(expr2) SUM(expr1) / n) / n, where n indicates the number of expression pairs (expr1, expr2). expr1 and expr2 cannot be NULL.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   If any argument is NULL, the function returns NULL. Otherwise, the return type is NUMBER.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   COVAR_POP(expr1, expr2)[ OVER (analytic_clause) ]
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM tbl1;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT COVAR_POP(col3,col4) FROM tbl1;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT col1,col3,col4,
            COVAR_POP(col3,col4) OVER(PARTITION BY col1 ORDER BY col2) "COVAR_POP"
          FROM tbl1;

