-- [F02-O0243] CUME_DIST
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/700.cume-dist-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/700.cume-dist-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: CUME_DIST
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   This function calculates the cumulative distribution of a value in a set of values and returns a value in the range (0,1\]. You can use this function as an aggregate function or an analytic function.
--   Note
--   As an analytic function, this function calculates the relative position of a specified value in a set of values. For a row row, assuming the rows are sorted in ascending order, the cume_dist of row is the number of rows with values less than or equal to the value of row divided by the number of rows being calculated (the entire query result set or partition).
--   As an aggregate function, the parameters identify a single hypothetical row in each aggregate group. Therefore, they must all be constant expressions within each aggregate group. Constant parameter expressions and expressions in the ORDER BY clause of the aggregate function match by position. Therefore, the number of parameters must be the same, and their types must be compatible. If a specified value is repeated in the grouping sequence, the two repeated values are treated as one value.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   expr | Specifies the value to query.
--   expr_col | Specifies the column name corresponding to the value to query.
--   DESC \ | ASC | Specifies the sorting method. This is an optional parameter. ASC specifies ascending order, which is the default. DESC specifies descending order.
--   NULLS { FIRST \ | LAST } | Specifies the position of NULL values in expr_col after sorting. This is an optional parameter. NULLS FIRST specifies that NULL values are placed before non-NULL values. NULLS LAST specifies that NULL values are placed after non-NULL values, which is the default.
--   OVER | Uses the OVER clause to define the window for calculation. For more information, see Analytic Function Description.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is NUMBER.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   /*Aggregate syntax*/
--   CUME_DIST( expr[,expr]...)
--     WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY expr_col [ DESC | ASC ][ NULLS { FIRST | LAST } ]
--                          [,expr_col [ DESC | ASC ][ NULLS { FIRST | LAST } ]]...
--             )
--
--   /*Analytic syntax*/
--   CUME_DIST() OVER ([ query_partition_clause ] order_by_clause)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM emp_msg;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT CUME_DIST(3300) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY sal) FROM emp_msg;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT deptno,ename,sal,
              CUME_DIST ( ) over (PARTITION BY deptno ORDER BY sal DESC ) "RANK"
          FROM emp_msg;

-- [F02-O0244] DENSE_RANK
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/800.dense-rank-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/800.dense-rank-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: DENSE_RANK
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   This function calculates the rank of rows within an ordered group of rows. The rank values are continuous integers starting from 1, and the maximum rank value is the number of rows that meet the query result. Rows with the same value in the specified sorting criteria receive the same rank, and the same number of rows will not be recorded in the next rank. It can be used as an aggregate or analytical function.
--   Note
--   As an aggregate function, DENSE_RANK calculates the dense rank of the assumed rows identified by the function's parameters based on the specified sorting criteria. Constant parameter expressions expr and order_by_clause in the aggregate function match by position. Therefore, the number of parameters must be the same and the types must be compatible.
--   As an analytical function, DENSE_RANK calculates the rank of each row returned by the query relative to other rows based on the values of value_exprs in order_by_clause.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   expr | The value of the corresponding column.
--   expr_col | The name of the column corresponding to the value to be queried.
--   DESC \ | ASC | Specifies the sorting order. This is an optional parameter. ASC specifies ascending order, which is the default. DESC specifies descending order.
--   NULLS { FIRST \ | LAST } | Specifies the position of NULL values in expr_col after sorting. This is an optional parameter. NULLS FIRST specifies that NULL values are placed before non-NULL values. NULLS LAST specifies that NULL values are placed after non-NULL values, which is the default.
--   OVER | Specifies the window for calculation using the OVER clause. For more information, see Analytical Function Description.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return value is of the NUMBER data type.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   /*Aggregate syntax*/
--   DENSE_RANK(expr [, expr ...])
--     WITHIN GROUP ( ORDER BY expr_col [ DESC | ASC ][ NULLS { FIRST | LAST } ]
--                           [,expr_col [ DESC | ASC ][ NULLS { FIRST | LAST } ]]...
--                   )
--
--   /*Analytical syntax*/
--   DENSE_RANK( ) OVER([ query_partition_clause ] order_by_clause)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM emp_msg;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT DENSE_RANK(3300) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY sal) FROM emp_msg;
-- Example 3: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT deptno,ename, sal,
              DENSE_RANK ( ) OVER (PARTITION BY deptno ORDER BY sal DESC ) "RANK"
     FROM emp_msg;

-- [F02-O0245] FIRST_VALUE
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/900.first-value-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/400.analysis-functions-of-oracle-mode/900.first-value-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: FIRST_VALUE
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   FIRST_VALUE() is an analytic function. It returns the first value in an ordered value list. If the first value in the set is NULL, the function returns NULL, unless you specify IGNORE NULLS, which is useful for data densification. The return value for rows with NULLs in the expr column is NULL.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   expr | The column name of the value to be returned. Notice: You cannot nest expr in analytic functions such as FIRST_VALUE().
--   {RESPECT \ | IGNORE} NULLS | Optional. Specifies whether to ignore NULLs. Default value: RESPECT NULLS. RESPECT NULLS specifies to include NULLs in the calculation. IGNORE NULLS specifies to ignore NULLs in the calculation.
--   OVER | You can use the OVER clause to define a window over the data on which the function operates. For more information, see Analytic functions.
--   SECTION: Return type
--   The return type is the same as the data type of expr, or this function returns NULL.
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   FIRST_VALUE { (expr) [{RESPECT | IGNORE} NULLS ]
--               | (expr [{RESPECT | IGNORE} NULLS ])
--               }
--   OVER (analytic_clause)
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT * FROM emp_msg;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT deptno,ename,sal,mgr,
              FIRST_VALUE (mgr) IGNORE NULLS OVER (ORDER BY sal DESC) AS first_mgr
      FROM emp_msg
      ORDER BY sal DESC;

