-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/970.audit-functions-of-oracle-mode/100.audit-functions-overview-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: Overview of audit functions
--   In Oracle-compatible mode, tenants can configure statement-level and object-level audit rules using the AUDIT/NOAUDIT statements. Audit log encryption is not configured via DDL; it must be implemented in conjunction with the audit_log_encryption parameter and the audit functions described below. The semantics of these functions are consistent with those in MySQL-compatible mode. When calling them in Oracle-compatible mode, it is recommended to use FROM DUAL.
--   Applicability
--   This content applies only to OceanBase Database Enterprise Edition. OceanBase Database Community Edition does not support the audit feature.
--   SECTION: Audit functions
--   The following table lists the audit functions supported by the current version of OceanBase Database in Oracle-compatible mode.
--   Function name | Description
--   AUDIT_LOG_ENCRYPTION_PASSWORD_SET | Triggers the archiving of currently writing audit logs and generates a new encryption key. This function is supported starting from V4.4.2 BP2.
--   AUDIT_LOG_ENCRYPTION_PASSWORD_GET | Used to determine the version based on the version number in the archive file name.pwd_idReturns the encryption password (or key material) for the corresponding archived log file. This function is supported starting from V4.4.2 BP2.
--   SECTION: Limitations and considerations
--   • The function must be directly and uniquely used as an output column (i.e., select item) in a SELECT statement, with no restrictions on parent expressions.
--   • Functions cannot be used in subqueries.
--   • You must have the SUPER privilege to use this group of functions.
--   SECTION: References
--   • Set up audit log encryption
--   • audit_log_encryption
-- Documented SQL syntax: no separate SQL syntax block on this source page; see notes and source.
-- Official SELECT example: none available on this source page. Use the preserved signature in a SELECT statement.

-- [F02-O0250] AUDIT_LOG_ENCRYPTION_PASSWORD_SET
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/970.audit-functions-of-oracle-mode/600.audit-log-encryption-password-set-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/970.audit-functions-of-oracle-mode/600.audit-log-encryption-password-set-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: AUDIT_LOG_ENCRYPTION_PASSWORD_SET
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   This function is used to trigger the archiving of the currently writing audit log file and generate a new encryption key when audit log encryption is enabled. Subsequent newly generated archive files will be encrypted using the new key. Typical use cases include key rotation or in conjunction with security policy changes.
--   Applicability
--   This topic applies only to OceanBase Database Enterprise Edition. OceanBase Database Community Edition does not support the audit feature.
--   Note
--   This function is supported starting from V4.4.2 BP2.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Privilege requirements
--   You must have the SUPER privilege to use this function.
--   SECTION: Description
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   This function has no parameters.
--   SECTION: Limitations
--   • Similar to other audit functions: it must be directly and uniquely used as an output column in a SELECT statement and cannot be used in subqueries. For more information, see the usage restrictions and considerations in Overview of audit functions.
--   • This function is only applicable to scenarios where audit records are written to disk files (audit_trail is LOG).
--   SECTION: Return value
--   The function has no input parameters and returns a string.
--   • When the operation is successful, the function returns OK.
--   • When the operation fails, the SELECT statement may still execute successfully, and the function returns an error message.
--   SECTION: References
--   • AUDIT_LOG_ENCRYPTION_PASSWORD_GET
--   • Set and view audit rules
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   AUDIT_LOG_ENCRYPTION_PASSWORD_SET();
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT AUDIT_LOG_ENCRYPTION_PASSWORD_SET() FROM DUAL;

-- [F02-O0251] AUDIT_LOG_ENCRYPTION_PASSWORD_GET
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Category: SQL function reference
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/970.audit-functions-of-oracle-mode/700.audit-log-encryption-password-get-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/500.sql-reference/100.sql-syntax/300.common-tenant-of-oracle-mode/500.functions-of-oracle-mode/970.audit-functions-of-oracle-mode/700.audit-log-encryption-password-get-of-oracle-mode.md
-- Documentation and argument notes:
--   SECTION: AUDIT_LOG_ENCRYPTION_PASSWORD_GET
--   SECTION: Purpose
--   This function returns the encryption password for the corresponding archived file based on the pwd_id (encryption key identifier) in the archived audit log file name. This password is used for offline decryption, forensic analysis, or integration with external tools in a controlled environment.
--   Applicability
--   This content applies only to OceanBase Database Enterprise Edition. OceanBase Database Community Edition does not support the audit feature.
--   SECTION: Syntax
--   SECTION: Privilege requirements
--   You must have the SUPER privilege to use this function.
--   SECTION: Description
--   SECTION: Parameters
--   • pwd_id: The timestamp suffix of the log file, which corresponds to the key identifier after the .enc. segment in the encrypted archive file name. This parameter is used to return the encryption password for the corresponding archived log file based on its timestamp suffix. If not provided, the password corresponding to the latest pwd_id is retrieved by default.
--   Note
--   This function returns confidential information. Strictly restrict call permissions and perform operation auditing and result desensitization. For the archive file name format, see the table in "Set log encryption" under "Set and view audit rules" at the end of this topic.
--   SECTION: Limitations
--   • Similar to other audit functions: it must be directly and uniquely used as an output column in a SELECT statement and cannot be used in subqueries. For more information, see the usage restrictions and considerations in Overview of audit functions.
--   • This function is only applicable to scenarios where audit records are written to disk files (audit_trail is LOG).
--   SECTION: Return value
--   The function returns a string.
--   • When the query is successful, return the password corresponding to pwd_id.
--   • When a query fails, the SELECT statement may still execute successfully, but the function returns an error message.
--   SECTION: References
--   • AUDIT_LOG_ENCRYPTION_PASSWORD_SET
--   • Set and view audit rules
-- Documented SQL syntax 1 (syntax diagram; substitute real identifiers and values):
--   AUDIT_LOG_ENCRYPTION_PASSWORD_GET(pwd_id);
-- Official SELECT example(s), cleaned of client prompts and result grids:
-- Example 1: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT AUDIT_LOG_ENCRYPTION_PASSWORD_GET() FROM DUAL;
-- Example 2: demonstrates the documented behavior above; required objects and privileges must exist.
SELECT AUDIT_LOG_ENCRYPTION_PASSWORD_GET(1001) FROM DUAL;

-- ============================================================================
-- [X01] Composition rules and compatibility boundaries
--
-- 1. Syntactic composition
--    Build a query block from SELECT, FROM, WHERE, hierarchical/group/window clauses,
--    ORDER BY, row limiting, and locking in the exact mode-specific grammar in R01/R02.
--    Parenthesize query blocks used as derived tables and give derived tables aliases.
--    A scalar subquery must return no more than one row; IN/EXISTS/ANY/ALL have different
--    cardinality and NULL/empty-set semantics. Correlation refers to an outer query block.
--
-- 2. Logical evaluation and names
--    Conceptually, rows are sourced and filtered before grouping/HAVING and final ordering.
--    Window functions evaluate over rows remaining after WHERE/GROUP BY/HAVING. Do not assume
--    a SELECT alias is visible in every clause; Oracle documentation explicitly permits aliases
--    in ORDER BY but not other clauses. Use deterministic tie-breakers for pagination.
--
-- 3. Aggregates and windows
--    GROUP BY collapses groups; window functions retain rows. ROLLUP/CUBE/GROUPING SETS
--    define different grouping levels. DISTINCT inside an aggregate differs from SELECT DISTINCT.
--    Window frames can change FIRST_VALUE/LAST_VALUE and moving-aggregate results.
--
-- 4. Set operations
--    Corresponding branches need compatible column counts and datatypes. UNION/INTERSECT/
--    EXCEPT/MINUS remove duplicates as documented; UNION ALL preserves them. ORDER BY/LIMIT
--    after a set expression applies to the combined result. Use parentheses to make nesting
--    and precedence explicit when combining several set operators.
--
-- 5. CTEs, derived tables, LATERAL, and APPLY
--    WITH names a query for the following statement; recursive CTEs require a terminating
--    recursive member. LATERAL/APPLY allow correlation to preceding FROM items; OUTER APPLY
--    preserves outer rows when the correlated source is empty. These are mode/version-specific.
--
-- 6. Hints
--    Use /*+ ... */ immediately after the relevant SELECT keyword. One hint comment per query
--    block can contain multiple hints. QB_NAME and @queryblock target nested blocks. Aliases,
--    not base table names, are normally required when a query aliases a table. Conflicting or
--    malformed hints can be ignored. Clients that strip comments can strip hints. Compare
--    EXPLAIN with and without hints; never use a hint to change expected query results.
--
-- 7. Conversion
--    Explicit CAST/CONVERT/TO_* makes target type and format visible. Implicit conversion follows
--    mode-specific comparison rules and can cause errors, truncation, rounding, NULL results,
--    unexpected collation/charset behavior, or loss of index efficiency. Formats and NLS arguments
--    must match input. JSON/XML/array/vector/spatial conversions have additional type constraints.
--
-- 8. NULL and three-valued logic
--    Comparisons with NULL generally yield UNKNOWN rather than TRUE. Use IS NULL/IS NOT NULL.
--    NOT IN with NULL in the subquery can reject every row; NOT EXISTS often expresses the intended
--    anti-join more safely. Aggregates and COUNT(expr) versus COUNT(*) differ on NULL handling.
--
-- 9. Locking and side effects
--    FOR UPDATE/LOCK IN SHARE MODE require appropriate transaction semantics and interact with
--    isolation/concurrency. NOWAIT, WAIT, and SKIP LOCKED differ. SELECT INTO variables expects
--    the documented row cardinality. SELECT INTO OUTFILE writes server-side files and needs
--    privileges. External table functions need locations, credentials, network access, and parsers.
--
-- 10. Validation checklist for a new combination
--    • Confirm tenant mode and exact 4.4.2 BP with VERSION().
--    • Check object names, column types, aliases, privileges, and optional-feature prerequisites.
--    • Start without hints; inspect EXPLAIN and actual row counts.
--    • Test NULL, duplicate, empty-set, overflow, invalid-format, and collation cases.
--    • Add ORDER BY tie-breakers before LIMIT/OFFSET/FETCH.
--    • Confirm set-branch column counts/types and window frames.
--    • For locks, test concurrent transactions; for OUTFILE, use a safe destination.
--    • Compare plans/performance before and after every hint.
-- ============================================================================
